@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.How-to-indicate-required-fields-in-forms a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "How to indicate required fields in forms";
    ds:summary "This guideline documents and explains the decision for marking required fields in forms, while taking into account WCAG accessibility requirements and examining various methods for indicating required fields in forms. ";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "# **Rationale**\n\nThere are various different methods to indicate required fields within forms. Some products denote the required fields with a “*”, some do it with text in the label, some denote the optional ones with text in the label, while others denote both. This creates inconsistency between products and even within the same product sometimes. Follwing this guide on when to use what method will provide a more consistent and predictable product experience for users.\n\n# Considerations\n\n## **Accessibility**\n\nAccording to WCAG 2.2 [Success Criterion 3.3.2](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#labels-or-instructions) and [Technique H90](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H90):\n\n1. *For each required form control, check that the required status is indicated in the form control's label or legend.*\n2. *For each indicator of required status that is not provided in text, check that the meaning of the indicator is explained before the form control that uses it.*\n\n\n\nIt can easily be interpreted as “marking all required fields” is necessary. However, many people requested clarification on the Success Criterion (SC), and an [official w3c Working Group response](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/3553#issuecomment-2022967832:~:text=which%20are%20optional).-,If%20some%20or%20all%20fields%20are%20required%20for%20submission%2C%20there%20can,above)%20are%20feasible%20and%20not%20ruled%20out%20by%20this%20success%20criterion.,-Other%20criteria%2C%20such) was provided. It is clear that distinguishing the required and the optional fields is necessary. However, some nuances are heavily debated.\n\n- Is it a failure of the SC to indicate required fields with an asterisk (*) *without* a legend to explain the meaning?\n\nSome Working Group (WG) members believe the answer is *no*, with supporting arguments like [this](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/4791#:~:text=But%20I%20would%20be%20concerned%20with%20creating%20a%20failure%20technique%20stating%20unconditionally%20that%20use%20of%20an%20asterisk%20without%20some%20kind%20of%20legend%20is%20a%20failure.).\n\n- Is it necessary to indicate required fields on *well-understood small forms where all fields are required* like a two-field login form and one-field newsletter subscription form?\n\nMost WG members believe the answer is *no*, with supporting arguments like [this](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/4791#issuecomment-3664703933).\n\n​\n\nTo summarize, here are the safe methods that should pass SC 3.3.2.\n\n- Indicating optional fields with “optional” in the label (by implication the other fields would be required).\n- Indicating required fields explicitly by adding “(required)” after the label.\n- Use a convention like the asterisk and legend to indicate required fields.\n- State “all fields are required” at the beginning of the form (if they are).\n- Do not indicate required fields on small, well-understood forms where all fields are required.\n\n## **Common form scenarios**\n\nThe word “scenario”, instead of “type” or “category” is used here because these are not mutually exclusive classifications. They are characterized mostly by intent or context of the form.\n\n### **Conventional small forms**\n\nThese are forms with very few fields (1-3), where all fields are typically required and the purpose is *immediately* clear. Examples: Login, Sign-up, Newsletter Subscription, Search.\n\nMarking required fields in these forms adds more cognitive load than value.\n\n### **Low-motivation forms**\n\nFor these forms, the user's motivation to complete the submission is often low. We are asking for more information than the user originally intended to provide, and because form submission is usually non-essential to their immediate needs, they are likely to abandon the process. This is common for things like customer survey forms and the majority of sites forms.\n\n​\n\nMarking optional fields may result in most fields being marked in some forms, making them more wordy than it should be. On the other hand, marking the required fields makes it clear the minimum information needed to proceed, which can arguably improve conversion rate.\n\n### **High-motivation forms**\n\nFor these forms, the user's motivation to complete the submission is often high. We are asking for essential information that directly facilitates the user's immediate needs, and because form submission is crucial to their goal, they are highly likely to complete the process. This is common for things like forms to complete a task or operation in apps.\n\n​\n\nUsers are eager to complete the form, so clearly indicating the required fields meets their expectation, gives them more confidence and promotes a smoother flow.\n\n### **Fully required forms**\n\nForms where every single field must be completed to proceed.\n\nMarking every required field may feel a little redundant, especially when the form is long.\n\n## **Consistency**\n\nUsing one method for all forms is dogmatic and unnecessary. Picking a method for each usage scenario seems to be more reasonable. We should also leave room for unknown exceptions as well.\n\n## **Position of the asterisk**\n\nWhen used to mark the required fields, the asterisk can be placed at the start of the label or at the end. \n\nThe industry convention is to place it at the end. Most users should be familiar with this position. On the other hand, placing it at the start makes it a lot easier to scan and find required fields without having to read the labels. It can be very helpful for users who value efficiency and transparency, which is true for most Canonical personas.\n\n​\n\nTable 10\n\n​\n\nOne thing to note is that wherever it is placed, the asterisk needs to be hidden with aria-hidden=\"true\" so that it is not announced by screen readers.\n\n## How to\n\nTable 11\n\n​\n\n# **Further information**\n\n[WCAG 2.2 SC 3.3.2 Labels and instructions](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#labels-or-instructions)\n\n[WCAG 2.2 Technique H90: Indicating required form controls using label or legend](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H90)\n\n​\n\nWG discussions:\n\n[https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/1698](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/1698)\n\n[https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/2357](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/2357)\n\n[https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/3553](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/3553)\n\n[https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/4791](https://github.com/w3c/wcag/issues/4791)\n\n  \nReference: [Spec DE077](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1FGtZ1ZOQ3QcYQ4-3nCh4jnycw7aeEN2l_XgmpFlFHq0/edit?tab=t.0)";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.How-to-guide.
